          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is COPD different from asthma?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Both affect the airways and cause breathlessness, wheeze and cough. COPD is mainly a long-term, progressive condition linked to breathing in harmful substances, usually smoke. Asthma is typically a variable condition, with symptoms that come and go and are triggered by things like allergens, cold air, exercise or viral infection.\n\n**Causes and onset:** The COPD reference summary says tobacco smoking is the main cause in high-income countries. Other causes include smoke from indoor cooking and heating, workplace dust and fumes, air pollution, and childhood lung infections. Symptoms build over years, often with chronic cough and sputum (phlegm). Many people attribute early symptoms to age or being out of shape, so the condition is often first recognised later. The reference also notes that alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, an inherited condition, is worth considering when COPD appears young or in a non-smoker. The asthma guidance describes symptoms of variable wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough, often worse at night or early morning. The retrieved passages did not say how asthma usually begins across a lifetime, so I cannot tell you its typical age of onset from this material.\n\n**Diagnosis:** Both are confirmed with spirometry, a breathing test that measures how much air you can blow out and how fast. [removed: this system does not diagnose.] For asthma, the guidance looks for variable airflow limitation, often checked before and after a bridging inhaler (bronchodilator). A normal test on a good day does not rule out asthma, because the variation is the key feature.\n\n**Classification:** In the US version of ICD-10 (the international disease code system), COPD is coded as J44 and asthma as J45, both in the respiratory chapter. Asthma is coded by severity (mild intermittent through severe persistent) and by current state (uncomplicated, with acute attack, or status asthmaticus). COPD has its own codes for related complications. The retrieved passages did not give a full staging system for COPD.\n\n**Getting help:** If you have sudden or severe trouble breathing, struggle to speak, or have blue lips, call emergency services now. Asthma attacks and COPD flare-ups can both be serious, and several other conditions cause the same symptoms.\n\nFor a personal assessment, speak with a doctor or nurse, who can arrange the breathing test.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
